pmc:ChaosMagicVmBDepressiveInversionOfEnthusiasmPattern a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Depressive inversion of the enthusiasm pattern"@en, "Depressive Umkehrung des Begeisterungsmusters"@de ;
    skos:definition "Depressive people evaluate a new idea by building an awful vision of themselves failing to enjoy it or suffering, then associate into it, feel dreadful and reject the idea; they cannot imagine what would do them good, and the process runs too fast to notice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 6 Access to Imagination, p. 84; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmp:ChaosMagicVmBActingRegardlessWhenAllThinkingIs a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Acting regardless when all thinking is negative"@en, "Handeln trotz durchweg negativen Denkens"@de ;
    skos:definition "When every thought turns negative, the best course may be simply to do something, whatever it is."@en ;
    skos:note "Strength in the source: may be."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 6 Access to Imagination, p. 85; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVmBHabitualEvaluationsRunTooFastTo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Habitual evaluations run too fast to notice"@en, "Gewohnte Bewertungen laufen zu schnell, um bemerkt zu werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "After years of living with one's brain, certain evaluations have become so quick and practised that one barely registers performing them; this is why the representations behind a choice or a mood go unseen unless deliberately slowed down and examined."@en ;
    skos:note "Added in reconciliation from the second extraction pass; the first pass carries no record of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 6 Access to Imagination, p. 85; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

