pmc:ChaosMagicVmBElderGodsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Elder gods and primal spirits respond to mood"@en, "Ältere Götter und Urgeister antworten auf Stimmung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Many gods and spirits care little for an invocation’s meaning and react faster to its mood, above all the primal spirits from the dawn of time, the ancient ones and the elder gods, who are indifferent to reason."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "elder gods", "ancient ones", "primal spirits (dawn ages)" ;
    pm:nameRole "mood-responsive archaic powers" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 94; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVmBCupMeditationAsPrayerAndCongruent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Cup meditation as prayer and congruent speech"@en, "Kelchmeditation als Gebet und kongruente Rede"@de ;
    skos:definition "Learned from two friends who had it from a Yoruba priest and were told it is a way of praying; Fries also names it a training in vivid imagination and congruent speech. Modelled on the eucharist: invoked force is charged into fluid, poured as libation and drunk to integrate it. He first thought it almost too easy."@en ;
    skos:note "Fries ascribes this to Yoruba priest, via two friends; the named source is reachable in its own right and is not read through him, so the node is entered as his ascription and not as a mediated reading."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 94; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVmBWaterChangesTasteDuringWorkingSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Water changes taste during the working"@en, "Wasser ändert während der Arbeit den Geschmack"@de ;
    skos:definition "Announced sign: water used as the fluid will change its taste in the course of the rite."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of Cup meditation (two-cup prayer); announced to the one practising and judged by the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 94; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmp:ChaosMagicVmBCupMeditation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Cup meditation (two-cup prayer)"@en, "Kelchmeditation (Zwei-Kelch-Gebet)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Take two cups or horns (ideally consecrated vessels reserved for ritual) and water, wine or spirits like rum or gin. Open with your usual ceremony, or simply relax, breathe deeply and sit silent for one to three minutes. Hold the full cup in the right hand and the empty one in the left. Pray, call and evoke a force or sentience; when it is strongly sensed, draw the vision into the fluid (for fire: inflame yourself with prayer until fiery and hot, filling the room with imagined flame, then shrink and intensify the vision into the fluid). Pour some into the left cup and invoke the next item, until the right is empty and the left full. Pour back and forth to blend, pour out a libation for the spirits and gods of your own mind, sprinkle drops around the room, give thanks and drink the remainder."@en ;
    skos:note "Fries ascribes this to Yoruba priest, via two friends; the named source is reachable in its own right and is not read through him, so the node is entered as his ascription and not as a mediated reading."@en, "The source orders these steps itself: Opening, successive invocations each charged and poured across, mixing, libation, sprinkling, thanks, drinking."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 96; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVmBSignsThatFireHasBeenInvokedSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Signs that fire has been invoked"@en, "Zeichen, dass Feuer angerufen ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Turning wild and fiery, feeling warm or even hot, eyes sparkling, laughing fiercely, the imagination filling the room with living flame; then the vision is ready to be shrunk into the fluid."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of Cup meditation (two-cup prayer); announced to the one practising and judged by the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 96; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

