pmc:ChaosMagicVmBAgainstDignifiedControllingCeremonialMageDispute a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against the dignified, controlling ceremonial mage"@en, "Gegen den würdevoll kontrollierenden Zeremonialmagier"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mages of recent centuries made total control the ideal, bowing to god while ordering spirits about in triangles and always sounding alike; this mask of the serene high priest comes from organised religion, which cannot joke or err; the usual ceremonial mage tries to master the elements rather than be congruent with them. Fries prefers undignified magick."@en ;
    skos:note "Dispute read from one side only: Fries against ceremonial magic tradition of the last centuries; the other side is not read here, so the node is a pm:Conceptualizing and not a pm:Disputing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 101; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVmBShamanicEnthusiasmAndPossession a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shamanic enthusiasm and possession"@en, "Schamanische Begeisterung und Besessenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamans grow wild and enthusiastic until spirits rise and obsess them; each spirit needs a different kind of enthusiasm, so the shaman must be flexible; incarnated spirits want to enjoy themselves and may provoke or insult, which is impossible if the shaman insists on control or dignity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 101; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVmBWildBoarAndSerpentSpiritsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Wild-boar and serpent spirits"@en, "Wildschwein- und Schlangengeist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beast spirits that seize the practitioner: the wild-boar spirit makes one enjoy snorting about on the ground; the serpent makes one lithe, sexy and venomous."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "wild-boar spirit", "serpent" ;
    pm:nameRole "possessing beast spirits" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 102; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmp:ChaosMagicVmBEmbodyingElementsBodily a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Embodying the elements bodily"@en, "Die Elemente körperlich verkörpern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Instead of mastering the elements, become congruent with them: grow strong and heavy with earth, sway and pulse with water, let heat, sweat and wild screaming come with fire, let air bring songs or breathing exercises; express their power and sentience in living flesh."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 7 Imagination and Prayer, p. 102; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

