pmc:ChaosMagicVmCFylgiaNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Fylgia"@en, "Fylgja"@de ;
    skos:definition "In German and Nordic lore an invisible guardian beast-spirit (named from following) accompanying a person, appearing as wolf, bear, lynx, dragon, eagle or lion; gives advice in danger through visions, dreams, omens, possession or divination; somewhat wild yet wiser than its human; some were skilled in spell-craft and worked synchronicities; in war some possessed their humans (going berserk)."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Fylgia" ;
    pm:nameRole "guardian beast-spirit" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 10 Waking the Beasts, p. 164; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

pmp:ChaosMagicVmCBerserkerPossession a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Berserker possession"@en, "Berserkergang"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being possessed by one's Fylgia in fighting rage, so ordinary consciousness is flooded by bestial rage leaving no room for the ego to doubt or flee."@en ;
    skos:note "Fries reports this of others; the node holds his ascription."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:VisualMagick ;
    dcterms:source "Fries, Visual Magick: A Practical Guide to Trance, Sigils and Visualization Techniques (Oxford: Mandrake of Oxford, copyright 1992; a later printing with ISBN 978-1-869928-57-5), chapter 10 Waking the Beasts, p. 164; read in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item JanFries-visual-magick-1992" .

